<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">progression</ansi>

Your level measures experience. Each level raises your Attack and Evasion,
the skill that decides fights (<ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>), adds a little maximum health,
and you gain skill training points every {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.TrainingPointsEveryNLevels" }} level(s).
{{ if eq (config "GamePlay.Progression.SmoothStatGrowth") "true" }}Smooth automatic stats spread growth across each level instead of waiting for
five-level steps. Whole points appear when the fractional growth reaches them;
levels 5, 10, 15 and onward keep their old automatic values.{{ else }}Automatic racial stats use the configured interval of {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.StatStepLevels" }} levels.{{ end }}
Gear, training and effects still matter. <ansi fg="command">status</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">experience</ansi> and the
level-up report show your next milestone, with planned features marked (coming).

You earn {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.StatPointsPerLevel" }} stat point(s) every {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.StatPointsEveryNLevels" }} levels.
Spend yours with <ansi fg="command">status train</ansi>. Companions automatically deal theirs
by archetype and focus (<ansi fg="command">help growth</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Health by archetype</ansi>

Health grows slowly: skill, not health, is what levels buy. Each class
starts with a head start and gains a little each level through level {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.HPFullLevels" }}:
  Warrior 10 to start, 1 HP per level; ranger 6 and 0.8; rogue 4 and 0.7;
  samurai 6 and 0.85; cleric 2 and 0.6; witch 1 and 0.55; wizard none and 0.5.
<ansi fg="command">archetype</ansi> lists each archetype's current rates.
After that, a ranger gains {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.HPAfterFull" }} HP per level, and each other archetype
gains in proportion to its rate: a warrior more, a wizard less.
Fractional gains accumulate across levels; each part of the total rounds down.
Add {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.HPBase" }} base HP, {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.HPPerVitality" }} HP per adjusted Vitality, and your health bonuses.
Without a known archetype, the early rate is {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.DefaultHPPerLevel" }} HP per level and there is
no head start. A level 60 warrior has about 88 HP before Vitality, half
again a level 1 warrior's 59 (<ansi fg="command">help health</ansi>).
Wounds reduce how far healing can restore you (<ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Promotion and talents</ansi>

At level 10 you promote to a class, and at level 30 some classes promote again
(<ansi fg="command">help promotion</ansi>). You choose a talent at levels 5, 15, 25, 35, 45 and 55
(<ansi fg="command">help talents</ansi>). The level-up report names what comes next.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Spells and abilities grow</ansi>

Damage and healing spells grow with your level and Mysticism: Magic
Missile does 8 to 13 damage plus 1 every 10 levels and 1 every 15
Mysticism, Minor Heal 10 to 16 health plus 1 every 6 levels
(<ansi fg="command">help spell mm</ansi> shows any spell's numbers). Opening Strike adds 2 damage
and 1 every 6 levels, Aimed Shot 2 and 1 every 3 levels, a guardian gets
a guard more every 10 levels, and from level 20 a tackle keeps its foe
down a round longer (<ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi>). The level-up report names each
spell and ability that grew. A level raises your maximum mana but
doesn't refill it (<ansi fg="command">help mana</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Experience and death</ansi>

There is no level cap. Through level {{ config "GamePlay.Progression.XPKneeLevel" }}, experience thresholds keep their
usual curve. Each next-level cost beyond that is multiplied by
{{ config "GamePlay.Progression.XPKneeGrowth" }} compared with the previous cost. Your race's
experience scale still applies. Level 100 is a practical ceiling, not a cap.

Death can cost a level. Your own trained stats and unspent points stay;
earning a lost level again never awards extra points. Companions' training
is derived from their current level, so a lost level can take its points
back and regaining it restores the same allocation. See <ansi fg="command">help death</ansi>.

Older characters keep their level, experience and invested training.
They receive the difference in earned stat points under the new rhythm
once, based on their highest level, including levels since lost to death.
Their racial stats and maximum health use these rules on loading; health
and mana above a new maximum are lowered to fit, without a free refill.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Milestones</ansi>
Wizards learn Shower of Sparks, clerics Minor Heal All and witches
Earthbind at level 3, kept for good once learned:
  Level 3: second class option (coming) for warriors, rogues and rangers
These later choices have arrived:
  Level 5, 15, 25: a talent (<ansi fg="command">help talents</ansi>)
  Level 10: class promotion (<ansi fg="command">help promotion</ansi>)
  Level 20: the advanced signature rank
  Level 30: elite promotion for warriors and clerics (<ansi fg="command">help elite</ansi>)
  Level 35, 45, 55: a talent, with elite talents for an elite
  Level 35 to 60: an elite rank every five levels
The elite step for rogues, rangers, wizards and witches is still planned
(coming).

